# Arduino Potentiometer-Controlled Buzzer

## Overview
This project uses an Arduino Uno, a potentiometer, and a buzzer to demonstrate analog input and sound generation.

Turning the potentiometer changes the analog reading received by the Arduino. The reading is mapped to a frequency, which controls the buzzer's pitch.

## Components
- Arduino Uno
- Potentiometer
- Buzzer
- 1 kΩ resistor
- Breadboard and jumper wires

## How It Works
1. The Arduino reads the potentiometer through analog pin `A0`.
2. `analogRead()` returns a value between `0` and `1023`.
3. `map()` converts the reading into a frequency between `200 Hz` and `1000 Hz`.
4. `tone()` generates a sound on digital pin `8`.
5. Turning the potentiometer changes the pitch of the buzzer.

## Arduino Functions Used
- `analogRead()` — reads the potentiometer's analog value.
- `map()` — converts the analog reading into a frequency range.
- `tone()` — generates a sound at a specified frequency.
- `Serial.begin()` — initializes serial communication.
- `Serial.println()` — displays values in the Serial Monitor.
- `delay()` — pauses execution for a specified time.

## Circuit Connections

| Component | Arduino Connection |
|---|---|
| Potentiometer VCC | 5V |
| Potentiometer GND | GND |
| Potentiometer SIG | A0 |
| Buzzer positive terminal | Digital pin 8 through a 1 kΩ resistor |
| Buzzer negative terminal | GND |

## Software and Tools
- PlatformIO
- Arduino framework
- Wokwi Simulator
- C++

## What I Learned
- Reading analog signals using a potentiometer.
- Mapping sensor readings to useful output values.
- Generating different sound frequencies with `tone()`.
- Using serial communication to monitor readings.
- Connecting input devices to output devices in an embedded system.

## Future Improvements
- Add a threshold that stops the buzzer when the potentiometer reading is low.
- Experiment with `noTone()` to stop sound generation.
- Explore other sensors and outputs.

## Project Goal
This project is part of my journey into IoT and Embedded Systems, learning by building practical projects with Arduino.


This directory is intended for project specific (private) libraries.
PlatformIO will compile them to static libraries and link into the executable file.

The source code of each library should be placed in a separate directory
("lib/your_library_name/[Code]").

For example, see the structure of the following example libraries `Foo` and `Bar`:

|--lib
|  |
|  |--Bar
|  |  |--docs
|  |  |--examples
|  |  |--src
|  |     |- Bar.c
|  |     |- Bar.h
|  |  |- library.json (optional. for custom build options, etc) https://docs.platformio.org/page/librarymanager/config.html
|  |
|  |--Foo
|  |  |- Foo.c
|  |  |- Foo.h
|  |
|  |- README --> THIS FILE
|
|- platformio.ini
|--src
   |- main.c

Example contents of `src/main.c` using Foo and Bar:
```
#include <Foo.h>
#include <Bar.h>

int main (void)
{
  ...
}

```

The PlatformIO Library Dependency Finder will find automatically dependent
libraries by scanning project source files.

More information about PlatformIO Library Dependency Finder
- https://docs.platformio.org/page/librarymanager/ldf.html
